        "id": "ch9_3-1",
        "text": "Introducing SELinux\nSELinux is a security framework in the Linux kernel for managing the access control policies of system\nresources. It supports a combination of the MAC, RBAC, and MLS models that were described in the\nprevious section. SELinux is a set of kernel-space security modules and user-space command-line\nutilities, and it provides a mechanism for system administrators to have control over who can access\nwhat on the system. SELinux is designed to also protect a system against possible misconfigurations\nand potentially compromised processes.\nSELinux was introduced by the National Security Agency ( NSA ) as a collection of Linux Security\nModules ( LSM ) with kernel updates. SELinux was eventually released to the open source community\nin 2000 and became part of Linux starting with the 2.6 kernel series in 2003.\nSo, how does SELinux work? We\u2019ll look at this in the next section. We will use Fedora 37 Server\nEdition for all our examples.\nEsta secci\u00f3n contiene las siguientes subsecciones\n\u2022 Working with SELinux\nSi deseas explorar cualquiera de estas subsecciones, puedes pregunt\u00e1rmelo directamente.",
